## Receiving results and retained failures

- The composed native suite passes **29 tests, zero skips**: 19 original
  tests, four answer-order tests and six reflection/actual-export tests.
- The PR #12 owner's unchanged notes browser receiver passes **15 checkpoints**
  and saves **six real text files**. Its seeded presentation controls retain
  canonical answer text and the same per-question order during practice.
- Rebuilding the demo from exact composed source in a separate directory
  produces the same SHA-256 as the executed standalone file.
- The original-main reflection source passed ten authored browser checkpoints
  and an independent five-check, six-download receiver. Those results stay
  attributed to their original source; they are not relabeled as this merge.
- The composed product passes **five independent browser checkpoints** and
  saves **six actual notes files**. Two simultaneous notebooks, completed
  mixed-outcome practice, edits and clearing, reset isolation and the direct-open
  demo with HTTP(S) blocked all pass. Every one of the eleven product source
  pins matches before and after both independent attempts. See
  [the independent PR #12 review](independent-pr12/README.md).

Three authored composition attempts are retained under
`composition/author-browser-failures/`. The first completed the native lesson,
then timed out on a CDP keyboard command. The second stayed on the static
welcome after keyboard Start and timed out before the first question. The
third waited for the native initialization marker and foregrounded its own
page; it completed the lesson, then timed out on a CDP evaluation. No product
assertion or page exception explained those timeouts, and product source pins
were unchanged. The shared filesystem was full during the later observations;
that correlation is not a demonstrated cause. The test-driver readiness
amendment did not close those author failures, so they are not reported as
passing composition tests.

The original driver is archived as
`composition/before-browser-preconditions.mjs.source`; the current driver adds
an end-of-initialization check and page focus before keyboard actions. The
native application calls `setProgress` after binding its Start handler, and
the resulting inline width distinguishes an initialized app from static HTML.
The independent receiver also preserves its first static-welcome failure: zero
checks and zero downloads. Its separately pinned successful successor adds only
two lesson-start waits, with all five behavioral assertions unchanged. The
exact patch, failed and successful runs, and source parity proof are under
`independent-pr12/`. Browser profile, temporary directory and output moved to
the reviewer's authorized private tmpfs for the successful run; product source
was unchanged. This success does not erase the three authored timeouts.

## Evidence custody and reproduction

Every file in the original 33-file qualification remains available, either as
unchanged current source, under `original-main/`, or as a `.source` archive.
`original-main/source-index.json` maps all original hashes, and
`original-main/original-manifest.json` is the exact original manifest. This
preserves the successful original source and its original missing-field
counterexample without treating the successor as having executed that history.

Run the native checks from this composed source:

```sh
node --test tests/*.test.mjs
python3 tools/make_demo.py
node tools/check_notes_browser.mjs --browser /path/to/chromium --output /new/notes-run
node tools/check_reflections_browser.mjs --browser /path/to/chromium --output /new/reflection-run
node docs/reflections-20261008-9ec02b70e5f0/independent-pr12/verify_reflections_receiving.mjs \
  --root "$PWD" \
  --pins docs/reflections-20261008-9ec02b70e5f0/independent-pr12/source-pins.json \
  --browser /path/to/chromium --output /new/independent-receiving
```

The browser drivers require Node 22+ and an existing Chromium/Chrome executable.
The reflection driver's output directory must be new and its parent must
exist. Read the actual result; the recorded authored composition attempts above
did not complete successfully in the observed environment. The independent successful receiver used Node `v24.19.0` and
`HeadlessChrome/153.0.8010.0`. Its five checkpoints are browser checks, not
additional unit-test methods. No dependencies were installed for these runs. The app and dependency-free native suite keep
their existing runtime requirements.

The two original reflection captures were inspected, and all saved-file
assertions read actual browser-written UTF-8 bytes. The owner's redundant
screenshots and duplicate GUID-named download copies are omitted; the named
files and raw reports are retained. These are local Chromium-family source
and consumer results, not learner-effectiveness, cross-browser, hosted or
contest-acceptance evidence.

## Upstream and next receiving boundaries

This delta is based on PR #12's exact head. Integrate it after that contribution
or stack it on that branch, preserving the owner's source history. Recheck
current heads before writing a shared branch. The reflection claim remains
[issue #5 comment 6057159375](https://github.com/Jacob-Met/RecallWeave/issues/5#issuecomment-6057159375).

[Issue #7](https://github.com/Jacob-Met/RecallWeave/issues/7) owns local deck
import and explicit session start. An accepted new course must initialize a
new notebook with the admitted question IDs and the receiving course's own
application prompt. Rejected/cancelled import attempts must preserve the
current session. This packet does not qualify that future importer composition.

[Issue #11](https://github.com/Jacob-Met/RecallWeave/issues/11) owns completed
trace save/preview/restore. Its initial stated archive scope excludes
reflection fields and preserves our notebook/export hooks. Explicit restore
must define whether a current notebook remains, clears, or is restored; old
writing must not silently attach to a different trace merely because question
IDs repeat. That receiver and its validation remain with its existing owner.
